A component with state and a func reading its own prop, instantiated once per
element of a list.

bubbletea keeps no state of an instance's own, so the component is spliced
per copy and its func hoisted to the owner -- outside the loop, while the prop
it reads was substituted as the loop variable naming the element. The func
read `it` where nothing binds it (`undefined: it`); it now takes each loop
variable it reads as a parameter, and the call site in the loop passes it.

html builds an instance as a factory closure, which is what the instance's
methods read their state through -- but a method kept the receiver parameter
the lowering gives it, and came out as `function describe(this)`, which
esbuild refuses; and a call to it from the instance's own body was spelled as
the page-level `row_describe(state)`, a function reading names only the
closure binds, so the first render threw.

deny out/none/html/index.html `textContent = row_describe\(` -- inside the factory the method is the closure's own function
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `describe__inst0\(__copy string\) string` -- the hoisted func takes the loop variable it reads as a parameter
deny out/none/html/index.html `function describe\(this` -- an instance's method takes no receiver: the closure is the instance

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component row(label string) node {
    var hits = 0
    func describe() string {
        return "{label}:{hits}"
    }
    button #b(text=describe(), @click { hits = hits + 1 })
}

window {
    var items = ["a", "b"]
    vbox {
        for var it = items {
            row(label=it)
        }
    }
}
